# qualified_optional.tkr — a NAMESPACE-IDENTITY project-regressor (0.3.1 regressor-principal,
# §5 triage exception): same-bare-name types (`aa::Thing`/`bb::Thing`) coexisting across
# namespaces in OPTIONAL and NAMED-VARIANT position (issue #109/#149) — each namespace's
# canonical `tk_opt_`/`tk_tag_` C emission must stay distinct. Flattening into one snippet
# would collide the two same-bare `Thing` types outright. Kept on disk, unflattened; not
# inline-scenario-izable.
#
# It is ALSO the host of two regressives that fbeaaa87 deleted while claiming "nenhuma cobertura é
# perdida" — the claim was false, `np_oop` and `native_gate_coercions` (#265) had no counterpart
# anywhere. They are restored here as MEMBER NAMESPACES (`src/np_oop`, `src/native_gate`) selected
# by `TEKO_REGR_ENTRY`, not as new projects: the owner's mandate fixes the regressive projects at
# nine, and a namespace is the language's own answer to hosting an unrelated fixture inside one of
# them. All three scenarios below share this project's SINGLE build (`RegrBuilt` compiles a
# project's `.tkp` at most once per `.tkr`) and differ only by the env selector — the
# one-build-many-scenarios shape the owner asked for, on disk.

#
# UNFOLD (2026-07-26, owner ruling "está proibido inline code em tkr"): this project is also a HOST
# for scenarios that used to be `Given source inline` blocks in regressor.tkr. Each is a MEMBER
# NAMESPACE under `src/`, selected by `TEKO_REGR_ENTRY`, and every one of them runs off THIS
# project's SINGLE build — the runner compiles a project's `.tkp` at most once per `.tkr`
# (`RegrBuilt`), and the members differ only by the env selector, args and stdin, never by source.
# The project's own scenario is unchanged and still runs from the same binary with no selector set.

Feature: qualified_optional — same-bare-name types coexist across namespaces in optional/variant position

  Scenario: aa::Thing and bb::Thing, both optional-formed and named-variant-formed, resolve independently
    When built and run
    Then exit = 22

Feature: qualified_optional members — regressives restored from the fbeaaa87 deletion, off the same build

  Scenario: np_oop — null-union narrowing over classes, interfaces and struct methods
    Given env = ["TEKO_REGR_ENTRY=np_oop"]
    When built and run
    Then exit = 0

  Scenario: native_gate_coercions — []T into [](T | null) param and field slots, and ~ on a u8 (#265)
    Given env = ["TEKO_REGR_ENTRY=native_gate_coercions"]
    When built and run
    Then exit = 21

Feature: qualified_optional members — KNOWN-STOP pins for the cells held back from the two restored regressives

  # A construct the restored `np_oop` fixture above WANTED and could not have, pinned rather than
  # dropped so that the day the stop lifts the pin fails LOUDLY and forces the direct spelling back
  # into `src/np_oop/entry.tks`. It is a CODEGEN-stage stop, so unlike the parse- and checker-stage
  # folds in the two compile-fail projects it cannot share a failing build with anything: a build
  # that reaches codegen is one whose checker PASSED. One build, and it is the only one this project
  # pays beyond its own.
  #
  # The two #265 `[](T | null)` cells are pinned ONCE, not twice: the parser refuses to spell the
  # type at all, which is the first wall and the one that has to fall first, and that pin folds into
  # examples/regressions/member_const_visibility_leak_rejected at zero build cost
  # (`src/pin_slice_opt_param`). A second pin behind it would cost a build to assert a `cc` failure
  # message that is not the compiler's own diagnostic.
  Scenario: KNOWN-STOP — a bare-null match arm into a `T | null` slot (np_oop held-back spelling)
    Given source = "cases/bare_null_match_arm_optional.tks"
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "nested bare-null optional"

